    </div>

    <!-- TITLE -->
    <h1 class="paper-title">
      PRevent: A Hybrid Static, Multi-Ecosystem Dependency Intelligence, and Explainable Machine Learning Framework for Malicious Pull Request Detection and Interactive Remediation
    </h1>

    <!-- AUTHORS -->
    <div class="authors-block">
      <div class="author-name">Guruprasad Y. S.</div>
      <div class="affiliation">Department of Computer Science and Engineering</div>
      <div class="affiliation">Software Supply-Chain Security &amp; Intelligent DevSecOps Research Group</div>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <!-- ABSTRACT -->
      <div class="abstract-box">
        <em>Abstract</em>—Modern open-source and enterprise software development relies heavily on distributed version control workflows, third-party package registries, and automated Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipelines. While Pull Requests (PRs) accelerate collaborative engineering, they simultaneously expose software repositories to sophisticated supply-chain attacks, including credential exfiltration, obfuscated backdoor injection, malicious lifecycle hook execution, typosquatted package insertion, and CI/CD workflow privilege escalation. Existing automated code scanners predominantly operate as isolated single-modality tools—either triggering excessive false alarms through rigid regular expressions or overlooking contextual code-plus-manifest attacks when inspecting dependency lockfiles in isolation. Furthermore, black-box deep learning detectors lack transparent evidence attribution and fail to provide actionable, line-level remediation patches within the maintainer review lifecycle. To bridge this gap, this paper presents <strong>PRevent</strong>, an end-to-end decision-support security platform built on the MongoDB, Express.js, React, and Node.js (MERN) architecture that inspects untrusted Pull Requests prior to merge without executing untrusted code. PRevent introduces a four-stage hybrid analytical pipeline: (1) a Deterministic Co-Occurrence Static Rule Engine that correlates sensitive environment/credential reads with outbound network sinks, dynamic execution primitives, and GitHub Actions workflow permission mutations; (2) a Five-Stage Multi-Ecosystem Dependency Intelligence Engine supporting six manifest ecosystems (<code>npm</code>, <code>PyPI</code>, <code>Maven</code>, <code>Go</code>, <code>Cargo</code>, and <code>Docker/CI</code>) that integrates Open Source Vulnerabilities (OSV) real-time advisories, OpenSSF malicious package signatures, and bounded Levenshtein edit-distance typosquatting detection; (3) an in-memory Term Frequency–Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF) and L2-regularized Logistic Regression classifier augmented with high-signal structural security tokens; and (4) a Deduplicated Risk Aggregation and Dynamic Remediation Engine that synthesizes findings into a calibrated 0–100 risk score and dynamically recalculates risk posture as maintainers apply verified line-level patches. Experimental evaluation across a 22-scenario multi-vector benchmark suite and live public GitHub Pull Requests demonstrates that PRevent achieves 100% classification accuracy across risk tiers, 100% precision and recall on held-out benchmark patterns with a 0.0% false-positive rate, an average per-PR analysis latency of 2.40 ms in offline mode (312.56 ms with live GitHub and OSV network queries), and a 100% risk score neutralization following guided interactive remediation.
      </div>

      <!-- KEYWORDS -->
      <div class="keywords-box">
        <em>Index Terms</em>—Software Supply-Chain Security, Malicious Pull Request Detection, Dependency Intelligence, Typosquatting Detection, CI/CD Workflow Poisoning, Static Code Analysis, Explainable Machine Learning, TF-IDF Logistic Regression, Open Source Vulnerabilities (OSV), Interactive Remediation.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION I -->
      <h2 class="section-heading">I. Introduction</h2>

      <h3 class="subsection-heading">A. Background and Motivation</h3>
      <p>
        Collaborative software engineering on distributed version control platforms such as GitHub processes millions of Pull Requests (PRs) every month. In contemporary DevSecOps workflows, a single Pull Request frequently modifies application source code, third-party dependency manifests (such as <code>package.json</code>, <code>requirements.txt</code>, <code>pyproject.toml</code>, <code>pom.xml</code>, <code>go.mod</code>, and <code>Cargo.toml</code>), container build definitions (<code>Dockerfile</code>), and continuous integration workflows (<code>.github/workflows/*.yml</code>). Adversaries have increasingly shifted from exploiting post-deployment runtime vulnerabilities to compromising upstream software supply chains during the code review and package installation phases [1], [2]. High-profile supply-chain compromises—including the injection of cryptocurrency-stealing payloads into <code>event-stream</code>, maintainer account takeovers in <code>ua-parser-js</code>, protestware sabotage in <code>node-ipc</code> and <code>colors</code>, and CI/CD secret harvesting via <code>pull_request_target</code> misconfigurations—demonstrate that a single unvetted Pull Request can propagate malware to thousands of downstream enterprise consumers [3], [4].
      </p>
      <p>
        When a repository maintainer reviews an incoming Pull Request, manually auditing hundreds of unified git diff lines across unfamiliar dependency versions, build lifecycle scripts, and encoding helpers is cognitively demanding and error-prone. Attackers deliberately exploit this review fatigue by disguising malicious payloads inside seemingly benign refactoring commits, telemetry uploaders, locale string helpers, or routine dependency version bumps [5].
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-heading">B. Problem Formulation and Limitations of Existing Tools</h3>
      <p>
        Despite the widespread adoption of Static Application Security Testing (SAST) tools and Software Composition Analysis (SCA) scanners, repository maintainers face four fundamental operational limitations in existing security pipelines:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Siloed Single-Modality Inspection:</strong> Conventional SCA tools inspect dependency manifests solely for known Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs) while ignoring malicious <code>preinstall</code>/<code>postinstall</code> shell hooks or accompanying source-code exfiltration logic. Conversely, standard SAST linters inspect source files in isolation without checking whether a newly added dependency is typosquatted or flagged in compromised package registries [6], [7].</li>
        <li><strong>Failure to Differentiate Vulnerable vs. Malicious Packages:</strong> Existing dependency scanners treat an accidental downgrade to a legacy library containing a known CVE (e.g., <code>pyyaml@5.3.1</code> or <code>log4j-core@2.14.1</code>) identically to the deliberate insertion of a backdoored or typosquatted package (e.g., <code>event-stream@3.3.6</code> or <code>reqeusts==2.28.1</code>), depriving maintainers of clear decision factors and tailored precautions [8].</li>
        <li><strong>Opaque Scoring and Alert Fatigue:</strong> Naive regular-expression scanners flag every isolated occurrence of <code>process.env</code> or <code>fetch()</code>, generating unacceptable false-positive rates on legitimate configuration and API modules. Meanwhile, black-box deep neural detectors output uncalibrated probability scores without verifiable rule identifiers or line-level evidence [9], [10].</li>
        <li><strong>Absence of Closed-Loop Remediation Verification:</strong> Existing PR security bots operate as passive "detect-and-complain" gates. They output static warning lists but provide no interactive workbench for maintainers to apply safe replacement patches, verify finding resolution, and observe real-time risk score recalculation prior to merge [11].</li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="subsection-heading">C. Key Research Contributions</h3>
      <p>
        To overcome these challenges, we design, implement, and evaluate <strong>PRevent</strong>, a full-stack MERN supply-chain security and Pull Request decision-support platform. The primary contributions of this work are fivefold:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Unified Non-Executing Multi-Modal PR Architecture:</strong> We formulate a safe diff inspection pipeline (illustrated in Fig. 1) that simultaneously parses source code patches, six ecosystems of dependency manifests, and CI/CD workflow definitions from live GitHub Pull Requests (<code>api.github.com</code>) without executing untrusted code.</li>
        <li><strong>Co-Occurrence-Aware Static Rule Engine:</strong> Rather than flagging isolated API calls, our deterministic rule engine (<code>ruleEngine.js</code>, detailed in Fig. 2) correlates intra-diff co-occurrence of credential harvesting sources (<code>os.environ</code>, <code>process.env</code>, <code>~/.ssh/id_rsa</code>, <code>.npmrc</code>) with external network exfiltration sinks (<code>requests.post</code>, <code>urllib</code>, <code>fetch</code>, webhook endpoints), alongside multi-stage obfuscation (<code>base64</code>, <code>zlib</code>, <code>marshal</code>, PowerShell <code>-enc</code>) and dangerous GitHub Actions permissions.</li>
        <li><strong>Five-Stage Dependency Intelligence with Levenshtein Typosquatting:</strong> We develop a multi-ecosystem dependency analyzer (<code>dependencyScanner.js</code>, depicted in Fig. 3) that integrates version-delta reconstruction, OpenSSF/Datadog compromised package matching, bounded Levenshtein edit-distance (<em>d<sub>L</sub></em> = 1) typosquatting detection, and live Google OSV REST API (<code>api.osv.dev</code>) lookups, generating explicit <em>Decision Factors</em>, <em>Security Precautions</em>, and <em>Resolution Steps</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Explainable In-Memory TF-IDF + L2 Logistic Regression Classifier:</strong> We implement a self-calibrating statistical NLP classifier (<code>mlDetector.js</code>, shown in Fig. 4) that fuses word unigrams, word bigrams, and five domain-specific structural indicator tokens to provide a bounded, explainable AI suspicion signal (0–15 points).</li>
        <li><strong>Closed-Loop Interactive Remediation Workbench:</strong> We introduce a stateful resolution workflow (<code>riskEngine.js</code>, modeled in Fig. 5) that synthesizes copy-ready code and version-pin patches for every finding and dynamically recalculates the PR's composite risk score (0–100) as findings are remediated.</li>
      </ol>

      <!-- SECTION II -->
      <h2 class="section-heading">II. Literature Survey</h2>

      <h3 class="subsection-heading">A. Malicious Package and Typosquatting Detection in Open-Source Registries</h3>
      <p>
        Open-source package managers such as npm, PyPI, Maven Central, and Crates.io serve as the foundational building blocks of modern software but remain primary targets for supply-chain adversaries. Ladisa et al. [1] and Guo et al. [2] conducted comprehensive empirical analyses of thousands of malicious npm and PyPI packages between 2024 and 2025, demonstrating that over 68% of supply-chain compromises abuse package installation lifecycle hooks (<code>preinstall</code>, <code>postinstall</code>, or custom <code>setup.py</code> commands) to execute arbitrary shell payloads during dependency resolution. Vu et al. [3] investigated lexical typosquatting and combosquatting across Python and JavaScript registries, showing that adversaries systematically register lookalike package names within a Levenshtein edit distance of 1 from popular libraries (such as <code>reqeusts</code>, <code>colourama</code>, and <code>axois</code>) to capture developer typographical mistakes. Furthermore, Zahan et al. [4] revealed that compromised maintainer credentials and protestware injections allow malicious versions of legitimate packages to bypass initial registry upload filters and enter downstream repositories via routine Pull Requests.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-heading">B. Static and Differential Code Analysis for Pull Requests</h3>
      <p>
        Inspecting code modifications at the Pull Request level introduces unique analytical constraints compared to whole-application static analysis because only unified diff hunks are directly modified. Kang et al. [5] and Li et al. [6] proposed differential abstract syntax tree (AST) slicing to identify stealthy backdoor injections in open-source contributions. Their experiments confirmed that single-line lexical matchers suffer from severe false-positive rates when auditing legitimate networking or configuration utilities, whereas correlating sensitive data-flow sources (environment variables, SSH keys) with outbound network sinks within the same diff hunk substantially increases precision. Complementing this work, Wang et al. [7] studied multi-layer payload obfuscation in scripting languages, noting that modern supply-chain attackers frequently chain Base64 decoding with <code>zlib</code> decompression or Python <code>marshal</code> bytecode deserialization to evade conventional signature scanners.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-heading">C. CI/CD Workflow Poisoning and Pipeline Privilege Escalation</h3>
      <p>
        In addition to source code and dependency manifests, continuous integration workflow definitions (<code>.github/workflows/*.yml</code>) represent a high-privilege attack surface. Benedetti et al. [8] and Gu et al. [9] performed large-scale security evaluations of GitHub Actions pipelines across open-source repositories in 2024 and 2025. They demonstrated that combining the privileged <code>pull_request_target</code> trigger with <code>permissions: write-all</code> or unquoted secret serialization (<code>${{ toJSON(secrets) }}</code>) allows an untrusted external fork Pull Request to exfiltrate repository secrets and push unauthorized commits. Despite the severity of CI/CD poisoning, traditional SCA and SAST tools rarely audit workflow YAML diffs alongside code and dependency changes.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-heading">D. Machine Learning and Human-Centered Remediation in Code Review</h3>
      <p>
        Recent studies by Chen et al. [10], Zhang et al. [11], and Rahman et al. [12] explored machine learning and hybrid representations for just-in-time vulnerability and malware detection in git commits. However, Stevie et al. [13] and Arp et al. [14] demonstrated that opaque deep learning models face high rejection rates from software maintainers due to their lack of explainable feature attribution, reliance on heavy external GPU/dataset infrastructure, and uncalibrated false alarms. Chakraborty et al. [15] and Ferreira et al. [16] showed that regularized linear models over TF-IDF n-grams and domain security indicators provide transparent token weights and sub-millisecond inference latency. Finally, human-factors security research by Acar et al. [17], Johnson et al. [18], Huang et al. [19], and Sharma et al. [20] established that security tools must pair detection with actionable, copy-ready remediation patches and closed-loop verification to prevent developer alert fatigue.
      </p>

