          <div class="figure-caption">
            <strong>Fig. 6.</strong> Quantitative comparison of initial composite risk score <em>R</em> (indigo/crimson bars), in-memory TF-IDF Logistic Regression suspicion probability <em>P</em><sub>ML</sub> (amber bars), and post-remediation score <em>R'</em> (emerald bars) across ten representative benchmark scenarios. Benign PRs (<code>TC-001</code>, <code>TC-011</code>) remain at <em>R</em> = 0, vulnerable-but-non-malicious upgrades (<code>TC-004</code>) receive moderate risk (<em>R</em> = 33, <em>P</em><sub>ML</sub> = 22%), and deliberate supply-chain attacks escalate to HIGH/CRITICAL before dropping to <em>R'</em> = 0 after interactive remediation.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        As demonstrated in Table II and <strong>Fig. 6</strong>, three critical empirical properties of PRevent are verified: (1) <em>Zero False Positives on Benign Commits</em>—<code>TC-001</code> (Python validator unit tests) and <code>TC-011</code> (safe <code>flask</code> and <code>pydantic</code> upgrades) exhibit low AI suspicion probabilities (6.0% and 8.2%, well below the 55% activation threshold) and receive an initial score of <em>R</em> = 0; (2) <em>Calibrated Separation of Vulnerable vs. Malicious Packages</em>—in <code>TC-004</code> (outdated <code>pyyaml==5.3.1</code> with <code>CVE-2020-14343</code>), the AI suspicion score stays low (22.0%) because no malicious exfiltration or hook tokens are present, placing the PR in the <code>MEDIUM</code> band (<em>R</em> = 33), whereas deliberate attacks such as <code>TC-002</code> (<code>event-stream@3.3.6</code> + <code>postinstall</code> hook, <em>R</em> = 85) and <code>TC-022</code> (multi-vector attack, <em>R</em> = 100) trigger both high deterministic category weights and &gt;96% ML suspicion; and (3) <em>100% Post-Remediation Risk Neutralization</em>—across every flagged scenario in <strong>Fig. 6</strong>, applying the suggested patches drops the recalculated score to <em>R'</em> = 0 (<code>LOW</code>).
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-heading">B. Ablation Study and Classifier Performance</h3>
      <p>
        Across all 22 benchmark scenarios (16 malicious/vulnerable PRs and 6 benign engineering PRs), PRevent achieved 100% tier classification accuracy. On the 16-sample held-out ML verification benchmark, the in-memory TF-IDF + L2 Logistic Regression classifier (814 active vocabulary features) achieved True Positives (TP) = 8, True Negatives (TN) = 8, False Positives (FP) = 0, and False Negatives (FN) = 0, corresponding to <strong>Precision = 100.0%</strong>, <strong>Recall = 100.0%</strong>, <strong>F1-Score = 100.0%</strong>, and <strong>False-Positive Rate = 0.0%</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Table III reports the results of our ablation study when individual modules are selectively removed, and <strong>Fig. 7</strong> visually contrasts the ablation configurations alongside the per-stage execution latency profile.
      </p>

