          <div class="figure-caption">
            <strong>Fig. 7.</strong> Dual-panel analytical evaluation: (a) Ablation study comparing detection recall, benign PR false-positive rate (FPR), and mean risk score error across five system configurations; and (b) Per-stage execution latency profile of the PRevent analytical engine in offline mode (2.40 ms total) versus live GitHub + OSV network query mode (312.56 ms total).
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        As illustrated in <strong>Fig. 7(a)</strong> and Table III, relying solely on the Static Rule Engine (Modality A) achieves only 68.8% recall (11/16) with an 18.4-point mean score error because it is blind to pure manifest compromises that lack inline scripts (such as <code>TC-004</code>, <code>TC-007</code>, and <code>TC-014</code>). Relying solely on the Dependency Scanner (Modality B) yields 37.5% recall (6/16) because it cannot inspect source-code credential theft or CI workflow tampering. A naive keyword regex baseline without source-to-sink co-occurrence (Modality C) flags legitimate environment reads and HTTP requests in 50.0% of benign Pull Requests. Combining Static and Dependency modules without the TF-IDF Logistic Regression signal (Modality D) detects all 16 malicious PRs but incurs an 11.5-point calibration error on multi-signal attacks. Only the complete <strong>Hybrid PRevent</strong> architecture achieves 100.0% recall, 0.0% false positives, and 0.0 points of score calibration error.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-heading">C. Runtime Latency and Interactive Remediation Efficacy</h3>
      <p>
        As detailed in <strong>Fig. 7(b)</strong> and Table IV, end-to-end runtime profiling across 50 consecutive PR scans on Node.js v22 demonstrates that PRevent completes a full offline hybrid scan in <strong>2.40 ms</strong>: 0.42 ms (17.5%) for unified diff parsing, 0.85 ms (35.4%) for static co-occurrence rule evaluation, 0.64 ms (26.7%) for offline six-ecosystem dependency and Levenshtein edit-distance verification, 0.31 ms (12.9%) for in-memory TF-IDF + L2 Logistic Regression inference, and 0.18 ms (7.5%) for deduplicated risk aggregation, with a total memory footprint of ~12.4 MB. When executing live network queries against <code>api.github.com</code> (168.40 ms) and <code>api.osv.dev</code> (142.30 ms), average end-to-end scan latency is <strong>312.56 ms</strong>, enabling instantaneous feedback during interactive maintainer review.
      </p>

